# Humanized UI copy

All user-facing strings in this project (dashboard HTML tabs, JS labels, empty states, tooltips, commit messages to users) must read like a person talking, not a spec sheet.

## Do not use

- Em dashes (`—`) or double hyphens as punctuation (`--`)
- Arrow glyphs in prose (`→`) unless they are literal UI affordances
- Middle dots as bullet chains (`·`) in sentences
- Stiff labels like "Primary KPI" when plain English works ("What we're measuring")

## Prefer instead

- Short sentences. Periods. Commas. "and" / "so" / "because"
- Empty values: `n/a`, `none`, or `not yet` (never `—`)
- Concrete outcomes: "When filtering is on, ctx strips about 4 tools per turn" not "Ctx direct effect: −4 tools"

## Examples

```
Bad:  "Extra prefix tokens are expected — you trade context for fewer back-and-forths."
Good: "Extra prefix tokens are normal. You spend a little context up front to cut redirect turns."

Bad:  "Profile filtering — tools stripped & token savings"
Good: "Profile filtering strips tools and saves schema tokens before each API call."
```

Apply when editing `src/dashboard_static/**`, embedded `src/dashboard.html`, and any dashboard-facing Rust error strings.
